

<p>I have always felt that the AI safety community should tell the world about AI risk a lot more. This has seemed to be controversial, at least in the parts of the community I’ve been in. Before about 2023, the common views seemed to be:</p><ul><li><p>it’s very hard to convince a person of AI risk</p></li><li><p>the best you will do is get half way and convince them that AI is powerful and near, without the danger aspects, and then they will try to form a frontier AI lab</p></li><li><p>The public is super irrelevant anyway</p></li><li><p>There is generally no way people as a whole will wake up to this before it’s too late</p></li><li><p>(And arguably, cynically: if people other than us start trying to influence the situation, it’s not even clear if that’s good—they might use their power worse)</p></li></ul><p>Well the public woke up quite a bit, and they are waking up more. So a lot of people became more optimistic about talking to them. </p><p>But still: </p><ul><li><p>people don’t do it straight, they tone it down a lot</p></li><li><p>they don’t think it’s very valuable (e.g don’t want to try to start a movement, more want to talk to policy makers than general public)</p></li></ul><p>Things to understand:</p><ul><li><p>this is the one thing we need (political will)</p></li><li><p>powerful people live in a soup of less powerful people</p></li><li><p>you don’t know who is powerful</p></li><li><p></p></li></ul>



